{"item_id": "dbe-mathematics-g11-p1-2015-nov-3.3.2", "source_id": "dbe-mathematics-g11-p1-2015-nov", "pair_source_id": "dbe-mathematics-g11-p1-2015-nov-memo", "subject": "mathematics", "grade": 12, "language": "en", "track": "core_maths", "kind": "question", "question_ref": "3.3.2", "marks": 3, "topic": "number patterns", "passage": "Consider the quadratic pattern: $5 ; 12 ; 29 ; 56 ; \\dots$", "question": "Prove that the first differences of this pattern will always be odd.", "memo": "$T_n = an^2 + bn + c \\\\ 2a = 10 \\implies a = 5 \\\\ 3a + b = 7 \\implies 15 + b = 7 \\implies b = -8 \\\\ a + b + c = 5 \\implies 5 - 8 + c = 5 \\implies c = 8 \\\\ T_n = 5n^2 - 8n + 8 \\\\ \\text{First difference: } D_n = T_{n+1} - T_n = 10n + 3 \\checkmark \\\\ \\text{Since } 10n \\text{ is even, } 10n + 3 \\text{ is always odd. } \\checkmark$", "final_answer": "10n + 3", "needs_review": false, "licence": "DBE copyright - check terms", "attribution": "Department of Basic Education"}
